# ALWAYS ON - operational journal
# 2026-10-01 - ao-grafana / ao-metabase login outage: KWallet readiness gate
# Session: Cline, coordination document E.
# Host: scottw-ms7b44, kernel 7.0.0-38-generic, systemd --user, Podman rootless.
#
# SYMPTOM
#   After the 15:08 reboot/login, ao-grafana.service and ao-metabase.service were
#   both INACTIVE (failed). Both are in the ao-admin domain and both materialize
#   secrets from KDE Wallet in an ExecStartPre.
#
#   $ systemctl --user is-active ao-grafana.service   -> inactive
#   $ systemctl --user is-active ao-metabase.service  -> inactive
#   $ journalctl --user -u ao-grafana.service
#       15:08:20 Starting ao-grafana.service
#       15:08:21 Failed with result 'exit-code'   (x5 attempts)
#       15:08:25 Start request repeated too quickly.
#       (grafana exits 1; metabase exits 3 = "KDE Wallet entry unavailable")
#
# EVIDENCE / MEASUREMENT
#   $ pgrep -a kwalletd6
#       3227 /usr/bin/kwalletd6                  # daemon ALREADY up at 15:08
#   $ systemctl --user status ao-wallet-bridge.service
#       OK: wallet-backed Mastodon env materialized   15:08:40
#       OK: wallet-backed sales database env         15:08:40
#   Grafana's first attempt was 15:08:20 -- ~20s BEFORE the wallet was usable.
#   $ ls -la ~/.local/share/ao-secrets/reporting-grafana-admin.env.tmp
#       -rw-rw-r-- 1 scottw scottw 0 ... 15:08:23     # 0 bytes, mode 0664
#   A 0-byte .tmp proves the write block never ran, i.e. hasEntry returned false,
#   i.e. the wallet was LOCKED -- not a missing file or a bad password.
#
# ROOT CAUSE
#   fetch-kwallet-secret.sh gated on the WRONG QUESTION. It waited for:
#       busctl --user list | grep -q 'org.kde.kwalletd6'
#   which asks "is the daemon PRESENT on the bus". kwalletd6 is D-Bus-activated
#   the instant anything touches it and appears LONG BEFORE the wallet is
#   unlocked, so the predicate returned true immediately and the intended 60s
#   wait never actually waited. The units then read a locked wallet, the
#   ExecStartPre failed, and systemd exhausted its 5 fast restarts in ~5 seconds.
#   fetch-reporting-env.sh (ao-metabase) was worse: it had NO wait at all.
#
# MORE
# FIXES APPLIED (both scripts, unprivileged, no data touched)
#   1. Replaced the readiness predicate with a real unlock test using the KWallet
#      D-Bus method isOpen(handle). Retained the 60s / 30x2s retry budget.
#      NOTE: isOpen has two overloads, isOpen(wallet,app) and isOpen(handle);
#      dbus-python binds the proxy to the LAST declared signature, so the
#      one-argument form must be used or it raises TypeError.
#   2. Added the same wait to fetch-reporting-env.sh, which had none.
#   3. Added `umask 077` to fetch-kwallet-secret.sh. It had none, so
#      "$OUTPUT_FILE.tmp" was created 0664 and the secret was world-readable for
#      the whole write block; only the final file got chmod 600. The sibling
#      fetch-reporting-env.sh already had umask 077 -- the omission was an
#      oversight, and check-secrets-exposure.sh could not see it because it
#      inspects final files only.
#   4. Added an EXIT trap to remove a stale .tmp on failure (installed AFTER the
#      arg check and OUTPUT_FILE assignment, since `set -u` would abort the trap
#      on the usage-error path).
#
# TESTS RUN (all with positive controls, per document B's methodology rules)
#   $ bash -n <both scripts>                                  -> syntax OK
#   $ fetch-kwallet-secret.sh                                 -> usage exit 1,
#       no "unbound variable" from the trap
#   $ fresh fetch output mode                                 -> 600
#   $ negative control: gate against a missing daemon         -> FALSE (correct)
#   $ positive control: failing fetch leaves no .tmp          -> no-CLEANED
#   $ fetch-reporting-env.sh grafana <out>                    -> exit 0, mode 600
#
# RECOVERY + VERIFICATION
#   $ rm -f ~/.local/share/ao-secrets/reporting-grafana-admin.env.tmp
#       (0-byte file, contained no secret)
#   $ systemctl --user restart ao-grafana.service   -> active; container Up
#   $ systemctl --user restart ao-metabase.service  -> active; container Up
#   $ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://127.0.0.1:3001/api/health
#       200   {"database": "ok", "version": "11.6.0"}
#   $ curl -s -u "admin:<value read from ao-admin/grafana-admin-password>" \
#          http://127.0.0.1:3001/api/health
#       200   # admin login authenticates with the wallet value
#   $ curl -s --max-time 20 http://127.0.0.1:3002/api/health
#       200   {"status":"ok"}
#       NOTE: metabase first answered 503, which was only its 18.3s init
#       ("Metabase Initialization COMPLETE in 18.3 s"), NOT a fault. Retried
#       after init -> 200. Do not read an early 503 as a failure.
#   $ systemctl --user list-units --all | grep -E '\bfailed\b'   -> empty
#   $ bash /ALWAYSON/scripts/validation/check-secrets-exposure.sh
#       OK: no secret-shaped content in tracked files   (exit 0)
#   $ find ~/.local/share/ao-secrets/ -name '*.tmp'      -> empty
#   $ stat -c '%a' on all materialized env files        -> 600, except the two
#       the bridge deliberately sets 0640 for the ao-sales consumer ACL
#       (mastodon.env, sales-db.env). Those are by design, via setfacl.
#
# SECRET HANDLING
#   No secret value was printed, logged or journalled. Verification used key
#   names, byte counts, file modes, HTTP status codes and sha/length reasoning
#   only. The admin-login check passed the wallet value straight to curl from a
#   shell variable that was unset immediately afterwards.
#
# NOT DONE (deliberately, needs operator approval)
